    {
      "id": 27,
      "type": "Note",
      "title": "START HERE | diagnostic test, not a verified replacement fix",
      "pos": [
        0,
        1770
      ],
      "size": [
        1650,
        590
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 6,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "Note"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "CONTROLLED PERSON-REPLACEMENT TEST - CURRENT H3 STUDIO v2 IMAGE\n\n1. Upload the existing scene in TARGET SCENE (Picture 1).\n2. Upload a clear face/hair photo in Picture 2; leave enabled ON.\n3. Upload the body reference in Picture 3; leave enabled ON. Crop out its face if that face must not influence identity.\n4. Leave protect_background OFF and click Run. No painted mask is required for this first test.\n\nSETTINGS: REF2VA INT8; Quality 50 steps; er_sde/simple; fixed seed; edit_megapixels 2.5; supporting references capped at 2.5 MP. There are no video or personal LoRA nodes in this test. No new models or rebuild are required on the current H3 Studio v2 image.\n\nREAD THE RAW OUTPUT FIRST\nA. RAW H3 RESULT is saved directly from Fizgig Decode, before any compositing.\nB. FINAL passes through the optional finish node. With protection OFF, it is the same image as A, not another render.\nThe side-by-side comparison is the resized source on the LEFT and the RAW H3 result on the RIGHT.\nAll saved outputs contain workflow metadata. Keep A (01_Raw_H3) for diagnosis.\n\nOPTIONAL SECOND TEST: paint a mask over the complete original person in Picture 1's Mask Editor and save it. Keep the same photos, prompt and fixed seed; switch protect_background ON. Compare A and B. Protection ON applies a final composite only. It does NOT guide sampling or remove the original person from the conditioning image.\n\nThis graph intentionally keeps the original scene reference intact for the baseline. It does not implement true masked inpainting or an opaque-filled conditioning reference. It tests whether identity replacement succeeds before the final mask can hide or clip the result.\n\nIn Replace person mode, edit_megapixels controls output size. generation_megapixels and aspect_ratio are not used to size this replacement; the target photo supplies the aspect ratio. Original photos are not overwritten.\n\nIf the raw result retains the original person, the next controlled comparison is removing that identity from the actual conditioning RGB image. A stronger prompt is not a guaranteed fix. GPU generation and likeness have not been validated for this diagnostic JSON."
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "text": "CONTROLLED PERSON-REPLACEMENT TEST - CURRENT H3 STUDIO v2 IMAGE\n\n1. Upload the existing scene in TARGET SCENE (Picture 1).\n2. Upload a clear face/hair photo in Picture 2; leave enabled ON.\n3. Upload the body reference in Picture 3; leave enabled ON. Crop out its face if that face must not influence identity.\n4. Leave protect_background OFF and click Run. No painted mask is required for this first test.\n\nSETTINGS: REF2VA INT8; Quality 50 steps; er_sde/simple; fixed seed; edit_megapixels 2.5; supporting references capped at 2.5 MP. There are no video or personal LoRA nodes in this test. No new models or rebuild are required on the current H3 Studio v2 image.\n\nREAD THE RAW OUTPUT FIRST\nA. RAW H3 RESULT is saved directly from Fizgig Decode, before any compositing.\nB. FINAL passes through the optional finish node. With protection OFF, it is the same image as A, not another render.\nThe side-by-side comparison is the resized source on the LEFT and the RAW H3 result on the RIGHT.\nAll saved outputs contain workflow metadata. Keep A (01_Raw_H3) for diagnosis.\n\nOPTIONAL SECOND TEST: paint a mask over the complete original person in Picture 1's Mask Editor and save it. Keep the same photos, prompt and fixed seed; switch protect_background ON. Compare A and B. Protection ON applies a final composite only. It does NOT guide sampling or remove the original person from the conditioning image.\n\nThis graph intentionally keeps the original scene reference intact for the baseline. It does not implement true masked inpainting or an opaque-filled conditioning reference. It tests whether identity replacement succeeds before the final mask can hide or clip the result.\n\nIn Replace person mode, edit_megapixels controls output size. generation_megapixels and aspect_ratio are not used to size this replacement; the target photo supplies the aspect ratio. Original photos are not overwritten.\n\nIf the raw result retains the original person, the next controlled comparison is removing that identity from the actual conditioning RGB image. A stronger prompt is not a guaranteed fix. GPU generation and likeness have not been validated for this diagnostic JSON."
      }
    },
